================================================================================
    SECTION 1: SMALL-CAP DAY TRADING FUNDAMENTALS
================================================================================

Small-cap day trading focuses on stocks priced between $2 and $20 with high
relative volume. These stocks can move 20-100% in a single session, creating
extraordinary profit opportunities for prepared traders.

WHY SMALL CAPS MOVE:
    - Low float (shares available for public trading) creates supply scarcity
    - News catalysts trigger outsized reactions in thinly-traded names
    - Short squeezes develop when bearish traders are forced to cover
    - Retail momentum creates self-reinforcing price spirals

THE IDEAL SMALL-CAP DAY TRADE CANDIDATE:
    Float: Under 20 million shares (under 10 million is ideal)
    Price: $2 to $20 (enough liquidity, enough volatility)
    Volume: At least 3x average daily volume in pre-market
    Catalyst: News, earnings, FDA approval, contract announcement
    Gap: Gapping up at least 10% from prior close in pre-market

TOOLS REQUIRED:
    - Level 2 market data (order book depth)
    - Time and sales (tape reading)
    - Real-time news scanner
    - Pre-market gap scanner
    - Hot key setup for rapid order execution

TRADING HOURS: The most profitable window for small-cap day trading is the
first 60-90 minutes after the market open (9:30-11:00 EST). This is when
volume is highest and moves are sharpest. After 11:00 AM, most small-cap
momentum fades and spreads widen.

================================================================================
    SECTION 2: THE GAP-AND-GO STRATEGY
================================================================================

Gap-and-Go is a momentum strategy that trades stocks gapping up significantly
in pre-market. The premise is simple: stocks that gap up on high volume with
a catalyst tend to continue higher in the first minutes of the regular session.

PRE-MARKET PREPARATION (7:00-9:30 AM EST):

    Step 1 - SCAN: Run your gap scanner to find all stocks gapping up more
    than 10% with volume exceeding 100,000 shares pre-market.

    Step 2 - FILTER: Narrow the list using these criteria:
        - Must have a news catalyst (check press releases, SEC filings)
        - Float under 20 million shares
        - No major overhead resistance within 20% of current price
        - Clean daily chart (not in a long-term downtrend)

    Step 3 - LEVELS: For your top 2-3 candidates, mark these levels:
        - Pre-market high
        - Whole dollar and half-dollar levels
        - Prior day's high
        - Daily chart resistance levels

GAP-AND-GO ENTRY RULES:

    ENTRY TYPE 1 - Break of Pre-Market High:
    Buy when price breaks above the pre-market high on the first 1-minute
    candle that makes a new high after 9:30 AM. Volume on the breakout candle
    must be significantly above the 1-minute average.

    ENTRY TYPE 2 - First Pullback Buy:
    If you miss the initial breakout, wait for the first 1-minute pullback.
    Enter when price holds above VWAP and the first pullback low, showing
    buyers are still in control. This is often the lower-risk entry.

    ENTRY TYPE 3 - Opening Range Breakout:
    Let the first 5 minutes establish a range. Enter on a break above the
    5-minute opening range high with confirming volume.

EXIT RULES FOR GAP-AND-GO:
    - Target 1: Next whole dollar resistance (sell 1/3)
    - Target 2: 2x your risk distance from entry (sell 1/3)
    - Target 3: Trail remaining with the 9 EMA on the 1-minute chart
    - Hard stop: Below VWAP or below the entry candle low

================================================================================
    SECTION 3: MOMENTUM SCALPING TECHNIQUES
================================================================================

When a small-cap stock enters a parabolic move, scalping techniques extract
quick profits from the rapid price appreciation.

THE MICRO PULLBACK SCALP:
During a strong uptrend on the 1-minute chart, price will make brief 1-3
candle pullbacks before continuing higher. Enter at the pullback low when:
    - The stock is above VWAP and the 9 EMA
    - Volume remains elevated
    - The pullback is on lighter volume than the impulse move
    - A hammer or doji candle forms at the pullback low

TAPE READING SIGNALS:
    - Large bid stacking: Institutional buyers placing visible support
    - Rapid prints at the ask: Aggressive buying pressure
    - Hidden orders: Large volume transacting with minimal price change
    - Sweeps: Rapid clearing of multiple ask levels simultaneously

VWAP RECLAIM TRADE:
When a gapping stock pulls back to test VWAP and holds, this is a powerful
re-entry signal. VWAP represents the average price for the day. Institutions
use VWAP as a benchmark, so reclaiming it often triggers algorithmic buying.

    Entry: First 1-minute candle that closes above VWAP after testing it
    Stop: Below the VWAP test low
    Target: Prior high of day, then new highs

AVOID THESE MOMENTUM TRAPS:
    - Extended stocks more than 100% above the daily 20 EMA
    - Stocks with halted trading (circuit breaker halts are unpredictable)
    - Stocks with no news catalyst running purely on social media hype
    - Any stock you arrive late to (if the big move already happened, wait)

================================================================================
    SECTION 4: RISK MANAGEMENT FOR DAY TRADERS
================================================================================

Day trading small caps is high-reward but also high-risk. Without strict risk
management, one bad trade can erase a week of profits.

THE 1% RULE: Never risk more than 1% of your account on any single trade. For
a $25,000 account, maximum risk per trade is $250.

POSITION SIZING FORMULA:
    Risk Amount = Account Size x 0.01
    Shares = Risk Amount / (Entry Price - Stop Loss Price)

    Example: $25,000 account, 1% risk = $250
    Entry at $5.00, stop at $4.75 = $0.25 risk per share
    Maximum shares = $250 / $0.25 = 1,000 shares

DAILY LOSS LIMIT: Set a maximum daily loss of 3% of your account ($750 on a
$25K account). When you hit this limit, stop trading for the day. No exceptions.
This prevents revenge trading and emotional spirals.

MAX CONSECUTIVE LOSSES RULE: After three consecutive losing trades, take a
mandatory 30-minute break. Step away from the screens. The fourth trade taken
in frustration is almost always the worst trade of the day.

THE THREE-STRIKE RULE: If you hit your daily loss limit three times in a single
week, take the rest of the week off and review your trading journal. Something
in your process needs adjustment.

SCALING INTO WINNERS:
    - Start with 50% of your planned position size
    - Add the remaining 50% only if the trade moves in your favor
    - Never add to a losing position
    - Your average entry price should improve, not worsen

GREEN-TO-RED MOVE: If a profitable trade reverses back to your entry price,
exit immediately. Do not let a winning trade become a loser. A break-even exit
on a trade that was once profitable is a win for your psychology.

Day trading small caps is not for everyone. It requires intense focus, rapid
decision-making, and emotional resilience. But for those who master the
mechanics and the mindset, it offers unmatched income potential from relatively
small account sizes.

